! Tetragonal nickel with a fully-relativistic PAW dataset: the anisotropy the
! force theorem cannot compute at all.
!
! **This cell is the point of the relaxed route, not a second example of it.**
! run_force_theorem refuses PAW by name, and correctly: its handoff from the
! collinear leg is a density and nothing else, while a PAW Hamiltonian needs
! ddd_paw, which is built from becsum -- a property of the WAVEFUNCTIONS of a
! run that used a different pseudopotential file with a different number of
! projectors. QE refuses it in the same place (potinit.f90:98). There is no
! handoff in a relaxed run: each direction converges its own becsum from its
! own states, so the refusal does not apply and the quantity exists.
!
! fcc nickel drawn on its tetragonal axes (a_fcc = 6.65 bohr gives
! a = 6.65/sqrt(2) = 4.70) and then stretched to c/a = 1.3, which is what makes
! the anisotropy uniaxial and large enough to measure against the noise of two
! total energies. At c/a = 1 the cell is cubic and the uniaxial term vanishes
! by symmetry, which is a control rather than a measurement.
&control
   calculation = 'scf'
/
&system
   ibrav = 6, celldm(1) = 4.70, celldm(3) = 1.30, nat = 1, ntyp = 1,
   noncolin = .true., lspinorb = .true., nosym = .true.,
   ecutwfc = 40.0, ecutrho = 320.0,
   occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'mv', degauss = 0.02,
   starting_magnetization(1) = 0.4,
   angle1(1) = 0.0, angle2(1) = 0.0,
/
&electrons
   conv_thr = 1.0e-10
   mixing_beta = 0.3
/
ATOMIC_SPECIES
Ni 58.6934 Ni.rel-pbe-spn-kjpaw_psl.1.0.0.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS crystal
Ni 0.0 0.0 0.0
K_POINTS automatic
3 3 2 0 0 0
